node_modules
dist/
build/
.next/
.astro/
.turbo/
.vite/
vendor/

# ─── Secrets / credentials (never commit) ───
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.json
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
id_rsa*

# ─── Logs ───
*.log

# ─── Editor ───
.idea/

# ─── Agent-settings runtime (never commit) — see #41 ───
# .claude/settings.local.json records pre-approved tool permissions, and
# those entries routinely name internal hosts, addresses and home-directory
# paths — the exact vocabulary this public repo must never carry. Until now
# it was ignored on exactly one machine, by that machine's *global* git
# ignore file (`git check-ignore -v` names the global file, nothing in this
# repo); that rule does not travel to a peer, a fresh clone, or CI. Measured
# on a second machine: the directory was present, untracked and unignored,
# so a single `git add -A` there would have committed it.
#
# The directory itself is deliberately NOT ignored below: .claude/ is also
# where a shared, reviewed settings file could be committed on purpose
# later, and ignoring the whole directory would make `git add` silently do
# nothing to that file too. Listing the local/runtime file patterns instead
# reaches every checkout by construction, leaves that door open, and still
# fails safe on a machine nobody has set up by hand yet.
**/.claude/settings.local.json
**/.claude/*.local.json
**/.claude/*.lock
**/.claude/scheduled_tasks.json
**/.claude/agent-registry.json
**/.claude/first-run
**/.claude/assistant-daemon-state.json
**/.claude/routines/.state/
**/.claude/worktrees/
**/.claude/checkpoints/
**/.claude/mailbox/
**/.claude/agent-memory-local

# ─── Local-only notes (by suffix, not by name) ───
# A few prose files here — working notes, migration notes, this machine's
# own CLAUDE.local.md — carry more than a permissions list: host names, a
# grant table. They stay out of this file *by name* on purpose: listing
# NOTES.local.md or MIGRATION.local.md here would publish that those files,
# and therefore that subject matter, exist, even though nobody outside could
# read them. A suffix pattern makes the same promise without naming
# anything, so it is committed instead: any future *.local.md is ignored on
# every checkout before anyone has to hand-repair it machine by machine.
*.local.md

# ─── Raw pane captures (by suffix — never a directory, never committed) ───
# testdata/corpus/README.md's curation path saves a raw capture to a scratch
# file before redact.go's RedactCapture ever sees it. That file must never
# reach git even briefly: this pattern is the second line of defence behind
# "keep it outside the working tree in the first place" — a suffix, not a
# path, so it catches a scratch file dropped anywhere by mistake rather than
# only the one location someone remembered to avoid.
*.rawcapture
